      "role": "user",
      "content": "i get that, but wouldn't you say the dissent's reliance on 'privacy' and 'autonomy' is actually a pretty weak legal argument since those concepts aren't explicitly written in the text? it seems like they're just trying to read new rights into the Constitution that aren't actually there.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Those who might argue that the relying on the concepts of \"privacy\" and \"autonomy\" by the dissent was weak could have some foundation, as the original text of the Fourteenth Amendment does not explicitly reference privacy or autonomy. The dissent in Dobbs v. Jackson, however, built upon a legal tradition that recognized these principles over time through subsequent Supreme Court interpretations. This includes landmark cases such as Roe v. Wade, which the Court deemed settled law - albeit not explicitly legislated - that utilized the Due Process Clause to safeguard personal liberties.\n\nCritics also cite the \"law of the judge,\" which suggests that the Constitution's meaning should evolve based on contemporary societal values and norms, rather than being strictly bound by the original text's interpretation. Proponents of this view might argue that the Constitution is a living document and that privacy and bodily autonomy can be derived from the fundamental principles of liberty and justice that underpin the Bill of Rights and Fourteenth Amendment.\n\nIt's also worth noting that the language of the Fourteenth Amendment, despite not explicitly mentioning privacy or autonomy, has indeed been interpreted to do so, creating a legal precedent. The dissent arguably seeks to challenge and realign the Court's role in determining which rights are protected under the Constitution.\n\nThis debate",
      "source": "target",
